.\" keyfilter(1) -- manual page
.TH KEYFILTER 1 "November 2026" "KeyFilter 0.01" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
keyfilter \- run a program in a pseudo terminal and filter the keys typed to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keyfilter
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B keyfilter \-\-discover
.br
.B keyfilter \-\-replay
.I file
.RB [ \-\-fast ]
.br
.B keyfilter
.RB { \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keyfilter
runs
.I command
inside a pseudo terminal and sits between the keyboard and the command.
What is typed is split into keys.
Rules decide for each key whether it reaches the command, is blocked, or is replaced by other text and keys.
The output of the command is shown unchanged.
.PP
Rules come from profiles, from options on the command line, and from commands sent to the running instance with
.BR keyfilterctl (1).
Every instance creates a socket, so it can be controlled while it runs.
.PP
.B keyfilter
is one file written in Perl.
It needs the module
.BR IO::Pty .
Nothing has to be installed to use it, it runs from where it is.
.PP
The first argument that does not start with
.B \-\-
begins the command, and so does an argument after
.BR \-\- .
Everything after that is for the command, including arguments that look like options.
.SH OPTIONS
Options that give rules can be repeated.
They are used in the order they are given.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " KEYS"
Block the keys.
.I KEYS
is one or more key specifications separated by spaces, see
.BR KEYS .
Same as the command
.B block
of a profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-unblock " KEYS"
Unblock the keys.
.TP
.BI \-\-map " KEY=REPLACEMENT"
Replace
.I KEY
by
.IR REPLACEMENT ,
see
.BR REPLACEMENTS .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Load a profile.
.I NAME
is a name, looked for in
.IR ~/.config/keyfilter ,
or a path when it contains a slash.
The profiles are used in the order of the options.
.TP
.BI \-\-startup\-delay " MS"
Wait this many milliseconds after the command was started, then send the startup text.
The delay is a number of milliseconds, no unit is written.
If it is given several times the last one is used.
.TP
.BI \-\-send\-startup " TEXT"
Send
.I TEXT
to the command at startup, see
.BR STARTUP .
.TP
.BI \-\-record " FILE"
Record the session in
.IR FILE ,
see
.BR "RECORDING AND REPLAY" .
.TP
.BI \-\-replay " FILE"
Replay a recording on the standard output instead of running a command.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
With
.BR \-\-replay :
do not wait for the recorded pauses.
.TP
.B \-\-discover
Show what the keys of the terminal send, see
.BR DISCOVERY .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-socket
Do not create the control socket.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options and exit.
It is looked for before anything else is checked, so it works whatever else is on the command line.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.PP
An option can also be written
.BI \-\-option= value\fR.
The value of
.BR \-\-block ,
.BR \-\-unblock ,
.BR \-\-map ,
.B \-\-startup\-delay
and
.B \-\-send\-startup
is the argument of the command of the same name in a profile, with the same syntax.
In particular a
.B #
starts a comment, a literal one is written
.BR \e# .
.SH KEYS
A key is written as one of the following.
.TP
a single character
.BR a ,
.BR Z ,
.BR 7 ,
.BR ! .
A
.B #
is written
.BR \e# .
Space is
.BR Space .
.TP
.BI C\- x
Control and a character:
.BR C\-a " to " C\-z ,
.BR C\-Space ,
.BR C\-@ ,
.BR C\-[ ,
.BR C\-\e ,
.BR C\-] ,
.BR C\-^ ,
.BR C\-_ ,
.BR C\-? .
.TP
.BI M\- x
Alt (Meta): the key preceded by Escape.
.TP
.BI S\- x
Shift: a letter becomes upper case, special keys get their shifted code.
.TP
names
.BR Enter ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Esc ,
.BR Space ,
.BR Backspace ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
.BR Left ,
.BR Right ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR Insert ,
.BR Delete ,
.BR PgUp ,
.BR PgDn ,
.BR F1 " to " F12 .
.B Return
and
.B Escape
are other names of
.B Enter
and
.BR Esc ,
.B PageUp
and
.B PageDown
of
.B PgUp
and
.BR PgDn .
Names are case sensitive.
.TP
raw sequences
Hexadecimal bytes, such as
.B 1b5b41
or
.BR \ex1b\ex5b\ex41 .
A token that is both a name and hexadecimal is the name:
.B F1
is the key, and
.B f1
is the byte
.BR f1 .
.PP
The modifiers
.BR C ,
.B M
and
.B S
can be combined in any order:
.BR C\-M\-a ,
.BR M\-S\-Up ,
.BR C\-M\-S\-F5 .
Special keys with modifiers are the sequences that xterm sends.
A modifier given twice, a modifier that has no meaning for a key, such as
.BR C\-Enter ,
and an unknown name are errors.
For a key that a terminal sends in its own way, use the raw bytes that
.B \-\-discover
shows.
.SH REPLACEMENTS
The replacement of a map, the text of a startup action and the text of
.B keyfilterctl send
are written in the same way.
Characters are sent as they are, except for the following.
.TP
.BI << key >>
The key, for example
.B <<Enter>>
or
.BR <<C\-a>> .
.TP
.B \en \er \et \ee
Newline, carriage return, tab, escape.
.TP
.B \e\e \e< \e#
A backslash, a
.BR < ,
a
.BR # .
.TP
.BI \ex HH
The byte with this hexadecimal value.
.PP
An unknown escape, a key symbol that is not finished and an unknown key are errors.
.SH PROFILES
A profile is a text file with one command per line.
Blank lines are ignored.
A
.B #
starts a comment, which goes to the end of the line.
Names are looked for in
.IR ~/.config/keyfilter/NAME.profile .
A name that contains a slash is a path.
A profile that cannot be found is an error.
An error in a profile is reported with the file and the line.
.TP
.BI block " KEYS"
.TQ
.BI unblock " KEYS"
.TQ
.BI map " KEY=REPLACEMENT"
Rules.
.TP
.BI include " FILE"
Insert the commands of another file where the line is.
A relative path is relative to the directory of the file that has the line.
A file is loaded once, a second
.B include
of it is ignored, which also ends include loops.
.TP
.BI startup\-delay " MS"
.TQ
.BI send\-startup " TEXT"
Startup, see
.BR STARTUP .
.PP
The commands
.BR send ,
.B toggle
and
.B profile
are accepted in a profile and do nothing there.
.SH RULES
Rules are used in this order: the profiles, in the order they were given, then the rules of the command line, then the rules sent to the running instance.
.PP
For each key all the rules that name it are evaluated in the order they were declared.
A
.B block
marks the key as blocked, an
.B unblock
marks it as free, a
.B map
sets the replacement, the last map wins.
At the end a blocked key gives nothing, otherwise the replacement is sent, or the key if there is none.
A key that is blocked and mapped gives nothing until it is unblocked.
.PP
Rules are looked at for the key that was typed.
Maps do not chain: with
.B map a=b
and
.B map b=c
the key
.B a
gives
.BR b .
What a map sends is not looked at again.
.PP
While there are no rules at all, what is typed goes to the command unchanged and at once.
Otherwise a key that is a sequence, like
.BR Up ,
is one key even if it arrives in pieces.
A lone Escape is sent after 50 milliseconds, when nothing completes it.
.SH STARTUP
The startup delay is counted from the moment the command is started.
When it is over the startup texts are sent to the command, in the order they were declared: the profiles in order, an included file where its
.B include
is, then the command line.
There is one delay for all the texts, the last
.B startup\-delay
wins, wherever it is.
Without one it is 0.
Startup texts are sent as they are, rules do not apply to them.
Text that was typed before the delay is over reaches the command before them.
If the command has ended when the delay is over nothing is sent.
.SH RECORDING AND REPLAY
.B \-\-record
writes a text file, one event per line, after a first line that gives the format.
An event is the time, in seconds with six decimals from the creation of the recording, a type and a text:
.PP
.nf
0.000412 O hello\er\en
0.812030 I ls\en
1.201553 X 0
.fi
.PP
.B I
is what the command received, after the rules, with the text of the startup.
.B O
is what the command wrote.
.B X
is the exit code, the last line.
The text is printable: characters from space to tilde as they are, a backslash as two, and
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.B \et
and
.B \ee
for the usual controls, any other byte as
.BI \ex hh
in lower case.
.PP
.B \-\-replay
writes the recorded output on the standard output at the recorded times, input events are not shown, and exits with the recorded exit code, or 0 if there is none.
.B \-\-fast
does not wait.
A recording that is not valid, for example whose times go back, is an error that names the file and the line.
.SH DISCOVERY
.B keyfilter \-\-discover
reads keys from the terminal and shows each one:
.PP
.nf
Key      : Up
Sequence : 1b 5b 41
.fi
.PP
A blank line follows each key.
A key that has no name is shown as
.BR (unknown) ,
with its bytes.
The names are the ones the rules use.
Discovery stops when the input ends, and when Ctrl\-C is pressed twice in a row.
Its output is made to be read by a person, and can be used by scripts.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
Every instance creates
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets/PID.sock
and an information file
.I PID.info
that tells the command, the start time and the profiles.
The directory and the files are private to the user.
They are removed when the instance ends.
.B \-\-no\-socket
does without.
If the socket cannot be created keyfilter says so and runs the command anyway.
.PP
A client connects and writes lines.
The language is the one of the profiles, limited to
.BR block ,
.BR unblock ,
.BR map ,
.B toggle
and
.BR send .
They are used at once, a rule for the keys typed after it.
.B toggle
turns a profile on or off, a profile that is not loaded is loaded and put after the others, a profile that comes back is where it was.
.B send
sends the text to the command, after what was typed before it.
No answers are sent.
A line that is wrong is ignored and kept as an error.
A line is limited to 65536 bytes.
Use
.BR keyfilterctl (1)
instead of writing to the socket.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/NAME.profile
Profiles.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets/PID.sock
The control socket of an instance.
.TP
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets/PID.info
Information about the instance.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Where the profiles and the sockets are.
The environment is passed to the command as it is, with its own terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B keyfilter
exits with the exit code of the command, or 128 plus the number of the signal that ended it.
.TP
.B 1
The command line is wrong.
.TP
.B 255
A profile, a recording or the command could not be used.
.PP
The signals INT, TERM, QUIT and HUP sent to keyfilter are sent to the process group of the command.
A change of the size of the terminal is passed on to the command.
When the standard input is a terminal it is put in raw mode, and restored at the end.
.SH EXAMPLES
Block Ctrl\-Z:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-block C\-z \-\- bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
Map Alt\-b to Ctrl\-t, and F5 to a command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-map M\-b=<<C\-t>> \-\- bash
keyfilter \-\-map F5=\(aqgit status\en\(aq \-\- bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
Profiles, the later one has the last word:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-profile common \-\-profile vim \-\- vim
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send a line to a shell after half a second:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-startup\-delay 500 \e
          \-\-send\-startup \(aqsource env.sh\en\(aq \-\- bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
Record, and replay without the pauses:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-record session.rec \-\- bash
keyfilter \-\-replay session.rec \-\-fast
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find out what a key sends, and use it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
keyfilter \-\-discover
keyfilter \-\-map 1b5b31353b3541=x \-\- bash
.fi
.RE
.PP
The directory
.I examples
of the source has scripts that run all of this without a terminal and without installing anything.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are written on the standard error output, starting with
.BR keyfilter: .
An error of an option names the option.
.SH BUGS
Two instances in one process are not supported, they share the SIGCHLD handler.
A terminal that sends a key in a way that
.B \-\-discover
does not know shows it as
.BR (unknown) ,
use its bytes.
.SH AUTHOR
Nadim Khemir <nadim.khemir@gmail.com>
.SH SEE ALSO
